Records the screen for seconds and lands the movie as ORDINARY MEDIA — File ▸ New Recording…, without the panel. The file goes to ~/Movies/Frame Job Recordings (or the folder path names), named Screen Recording <date> at <time>.mov, and is then imported as import_media imports a file — a media entry — and, with place (the default), placed at the playhead on the front track of whatever container is open (inside a nest when set_scope has one open), or, when that track has no room there, on a new track in front of everything; a paused recording lands one clip per segment (below). With no project open, or when the target document closed while the recording ran, it opens as a NEW untitled project instead and landed says new_project; that document is then addressable by project: <the recording's path>, which is the path get_project reports for it. The canvas of that new project takes rate, while the media record keeps the file's OWN frame rate — for a screen recording that is whatever the screen did, since ScreenCaptureKit delivers a frame only when something changes, so a mostly-still screen reports a low, fractional fps; that is the file, not a defect, and the picture holds its last frame to the end of the sound. A microphone is a SECOND file (<stem> (Microphone).m4a) landing as a second clip on a new back track named Microphone, at the picture's start minus mic_offset — the measured lead of one file over the other, in ticks, positive meaning the picture began after the microphone; a negative start is the head trimmed, not a clip placed late. A microphone that never started, whose writer failed or whose file will not read does NOT fail the recording: the picture lands, that segment's mic_path and mic ids are absent, and mic_problem carries the sentence (the note repeats it). THE GRANTS: this tool never asks for one and never opens the picker. The Screen Recording grant (and the Microphone grant when microphone is true, and the Camera grant for source camera or a camera beside the screen) must already be held by this process — the panel is where somebody grants them — and a missing one is refused by name with the System Settings pane in the sentence. A Frame Job launched from a shell runs under the TERMINAL's grants, not the app icon's, and both refusals then also name the launching process (its pid) and the way out — relaunch with open -a "Frame Job", or grant the terminal. THE REFUSAL ORDER is fixed: your own mistakes first (seconds missing or outside 1…600, a source outside the vocabulary and the arguments that source refuses, display and window both given, a rate or size outside the vocabulary, enhance: true without a microphone and then with place: false, auto_zoom, shortcut_overlays and speed_up_typing each with place: false and the last two without keys: shortcuts, a camera_layout outside the vocabulary, camera_layout and then camera_cutout: true with place: false, microphone: true without the Microphone grant, a project naming nothing open), t
